<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <RootNamespace>Ruledger.Cli</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>False</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1605</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The one thing v1 publishes. The library beside it is not packable and is not meant to be:
    what Ruledger offers is a command, and a published API would be a second surface to keep
    still. The package still carries the .Cli suffix the ecosystem uses, because the package
    identifier and the project it holds may not be the same name.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>ruledger</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>Ruledger.Cli</PackageId>
    <Version>1.0.1</Version>
    <Authors>Reny</Authors>
    <Description>Walks a Rulealize rule set and writes down, for every state it visits, everything that can be observed there: which inputs are legal, whether it is final and with what result, and where each legal input leads. Applying that test design to a later version of the rule set is the diff, and the choices a person made are carried across it.</Description>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/reny-develop/Ruledger</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The runtime, for the same two calls the walk is made of, and for what a document says it
    holds. Reading `uses` through Rulealize rather than out of the JSON here is the same
    decision Rulealize.Cli made: a second reading of it would eventually disagree with the
    one that compiles the document, and the way it would show is this tool handing over a
    set of components the runtime then refuses.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Rulealize" Version="0.8.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ruledger\Ruledger.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    So that what the tool prints can be held against what the documents say it prints. A
    command is a class here rather than a script for that reason: the exit code and the
    lines are the whole of the interface, and both are checked.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Ruledger.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
